    "aspects": [
      {
        "id": "check-then-act",
        "says": "A thread-safe collection does not make a check-then-act sequence atomic; putIfAbsent does the check and the put as one step.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember",
          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them"
        ],
        "exercise": "claim",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "chm-size-stale",
        "says": "ConcurrentHashMap.size() may be stale while other threads change the map, and mappingCount() gives a long count.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Edge Cases and Their Handling"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "coarse-vs-fine-locking",
        "says": "A synchronized wrapper takes one lock for every call, while ConcurrentHashMap locks per bin, so it scales far better under contention.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Concept Explanation",
          "section:Interview-specific Insights"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "a throughput claim no deterministic test can observe; the locking model behind it is asked in page 6.5.1's quiz (q-chm-concurrency)"
      },
      {
        "id": "compute-if-absent",
        "says": "computeIfAbsent initializes a missing value atomically, so two threads never create and store two values for one key.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Best Practices and Optimization Techniques"
        ],
        "exercise": "groups",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "cow-vs-synchronized-list",
        "says": "CopyOnWriteArrayList fits a small list read far more often than written; a synchronized list fits balanced or write-heavy use.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section",
          "section:Edge Cases and Their Handling"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "high-contention-counters",
        "says": "AtomicInteger or LongAdder values in a ConcurrentHashMap raise a counter's throughput under heavy contention.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section",
          "section:Best Practices and Optimization Techniques"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "a throughput choice; the correctness it keeps, no lost update, is what the counter practice checks with merge"
      },
      {
        "id": "legacy-collections",
        "says": "Vector and Hashtable synchronize every method, still need external locking for compound sequences, and are replaced by ArrayList, HashMap and the concurrent collections.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "legacy classes the page advises against; the flaw it names, a non-atomic compound sequence, is the one the claim practice checks"
      },
      {
        "id": "read-modify-write",
        "says": "get followed by put is two atomic calls, not one; a concurrent counter must use an atomic read-modify-write such as merge or compute, or updates are lost.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them",
          "section:Interview Q&A Section"
        ],
        "exercise": "counter",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "serialization",
        "says": "Synchronized wrappers and concurrent collections are serializable, a wrapper only when its backing collection is.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Edge Cases and Their Handling"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "incidental detail, not practised"
      },
      {
        "id": "virtual-thread-pinning",
        "says": "In Java 21, synchronized blocks inside virtual-thread tasks pin the carrier thread.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Relevant Java 21 Features"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "a scheduling property no graded test can observe; virtual threads are the subject of module 26"
      },
      {
        "id": "wrapper-iteration",
        "says": "A synchronized wrapper locks each call but not an iteration, so a walk over it must hold the wrapper's own lock or work on a copy taken under it.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them",
